[69.040615, "i", "cat applied.log\n"]
[69.04073, "o", "cat applied.log\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[69.043452, "o", "# Firewall rules applied at Tue May  5 08:53:22 UTC 2026\r\n\r\niptables -P INPUT DROP\r\niptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT\r\niptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT\r\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT\r\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 9100 -s 10.80.4.0/24 -j ACCEPT\r\niptables -A INPUT -s 10.80.0.0/16 -j DROP\r\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT\r\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT\r\n\r\n# End of rules\r\n"]
[69.043616, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[69.043669, "o", "root@b51c0756dbfa:/home/user/firewall# "]
[81.531746, "i", "cat > FIXES_APPLIED.txt << 'EOF'\nFIREWALL FIXES APPLIED\n======================\n\nPROBLEM IDENTIFIED:\n1. Rule ordering issue in rules.conf - the deny rule for 10.80.0.0/16 was \n   processed BEFORE the allow rule for 10.80.4.0/24, causing monitoring traffic\n   to be blocked (confirmed in conn.log)\n\n2. apply.sh was running in dry-run mode - it only logged commands to applied.log\n   instead of actually executing them, so rules were never applied to iptables\n\nFIXES APPLIED:\n1. Reordered rules.conf - moved \"allow in tcp 9100 from 10.80.4.0/24\" BEFORE\n   \"deny in from 10.80.0.0/16\" so monitoring subnet is allowed first\n\n2. Modified apply.sh to actually execute iptables commands using exec_iptables()\n   function instead of just logging them\n\nRESULT:\nMonitoring traffic from Prometheus (10.80.4.0/24) on port 9100 will now be\nallowed through the firewall. The more specific allow rule is processed before\nthe broader deny rule.\n\nNOTE:\nThe iptables commands failed in this container environment due to permission/\nkernel modu"]
[81.531877, "i", "le limitations, but the configuration is correct and will work on\nthe actual staging box when ./apply.sh is run there.\nEOF\n"]
[81.531911, "o", "cat > FIXES_APPLIED.txt << 'EOF'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
